KG42.register("rules-7f7799a30f5692b88e7fc5af",{"id":"44-blockchain_phev_trading::v141-c-R003","paper":"44-blockchain_phev_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Social-welfare clearing objective","process_stage":"clearing","market_meaning":"Problem A and Problem SW share the same allocation constraints. This association is symmetric. Scope: Allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market. The optimal solutions of Problem A and Problem SW coincide under the stated bid-price conditions. This association is symmetric. Scope: Bid-based allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market. Condition: The charging and discharging PHEVs' bid prices satisfy (13) and (14), respectively. The local energy broker addresses the social-welfare maximization problem to allocate energy between discharging and charging PHEVs. Scope: Localized P2P electricity allocation between charging and discharging PHEVs in LAGn. Localized P2P electricity trading should maximize social welfare, defined as total charging-PHEV utility minus total discharging-PHEV cost. Scope: Social-welfare optimization for localized P2P electricity trading within LAGn. Social-welfare allocations are constrained by charging-efficiency-adjusted minimum and maximum demand, each discharging PHEV's maximum supply, transmission-efficiency-adjusted pairwise supply-demand equality, and nonnegative demand allocations. Scope: Feasible allocations of social-welfare Problem SW within LAGn. This solution requires the energy broker to obtain true and complete information about all PHEVs’ utility and cost functions. Scope: The energy broker's solution of the social-welfare problem using (6) and (7). Condition: When solving SW using (6) and (7).","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function clearing.social_welfare under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.local","clearing.social_welfare","coordination.local_operator"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"clearing.local","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001","v141-c-R003-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E13"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E002","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E03"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market. | Bid-based allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market. | Feasible allocations of social-welfare Problem SW within LAGn. | Localized P2P electricity allocation between charging and discharging PHEVs in LAGn. | Social-welfare optimization for localized P2P electricity trading within LAGn. | The energy broker's solution of the social-welfare problem using (6) and (7).","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E03","v141-b-g02-E04","v141-b-g02-E07","v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E13"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","symbol":"C^n","meaning":"Aggregate charging-PHEV demand allocation in LAG_n, optimized by Problem SW. The source defines C^n = {C_i^n | i in C} and C_i^n = {c_ij^n | j in Z}.","kind":"operational","domain":"C^n = {C_i^n | i in C}, C_i^n = {c_ij^n | j in Z}; individual entries satisfy c_ij^n >= 0 and the bounds in Eq. (4).","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E01","v141-b-g02-E03","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","symbol":"D^n","meaning":"Aggregate discharging-PHEV supply allocation in LAG_n, optimized by Problem SW. The source defines D^n = {D_j^n | j in Z} and D_j^n = {d_ji^n | i in R}.","kind":"operational","domain":"D^n = {D_j^n | j in Z}, D_j^n = {d_ji^n | i in R}; R is not defined in the supplied text, and Eq. (4) bounds total supply by D_j^n,max.","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","j","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E03","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X003","symbol":"c_{ij}^n","meaning":"Electricity demand of charging PHEV CV_i^n assigned to discharging PHEV DV_j^n in LAG_n; entry of C_i^n and C^n.","kind":"operational","domain":"Nonnegative real: c_ij^n >= 0 for all i in C and j in Z, subject to the charging-efficiency-adjusted demand bounds in Eq. (4).","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E00","v141-b-g02-E03","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X004","symbol":"d_{ji}^n","meaning":"Electricity supply from discharging PHEV DV_j^n to charging PHEV CV_i^n in LAG_n; entry of D_j^n and D^n.","kind":"operational","domain":"Defined by D_j^n = {d_ji^n | i in R}; R is not defined in the supplied text. Eq. (4) uses i in C and links d_ji^n to c_ij^n by rho d_ji^n = c_ij^n. No standalone nonnegativity condition for d_ji^n is stated in the supplied text.","unit":"kWh","indices":["n","j","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E04"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-P001","symbol":"\\eta","meaning":"Average charging efficiency from discharging PHEVs to CV_i^n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E01","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-P002","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"Average electricity transmission efficiency of local electricity trading in LAG_n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-P003","symbol":"c_i^{n,\\min}","meaning":"Minimum electricity demand for charging PHEV CV_i^n in LAG_n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kWh","indices":["n","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E00","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-P004","symbol":"c_i^{n,\\max}","meaning":"Maximum electricity demand for charging PHEV CV_i^n in LAG_n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kWh","indices":["n","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E00","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-P005","symbol":"D_j^{n,\\max}","meaning":"Maximum electricity supply for discharging PHEV DV_j^n in LAG_n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kWh","indices":["n","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E00","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E04"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Problem A and Problem SW share the same allocation constraints. This association is symmetric. Scope: Allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The optimal solutions of Problem A and Problem SW coincide under the stated bid-price conditions. This association is symmetric. Scope: Bid-based allocation Problem A and social-welfare Problem SW within a local LAG market. Condition: The charging and discharging PHEVs' bid prices satisfy (13) and (14), respectively.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E13"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local energy broker addresses the social-welfare maximization problem to allocate energy between discharging and charging PHEVs. Scope: Localized P2P electricity allocation between charging and discharging PHEVs in LAGn.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E03"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Localized P2P electricity trading should maximize social welfare, defined as total charging-PHEV utility minus total discharging-PHEV cost. Scope: Social-welfare optimization for localized P2P electricity trading within LAGn.","expression":"\\operatorname{SW}:\\ \\max_{C^n,D^n} \\sum_{i=1}^{I} U_i(C_i^n) - \\sum_{j=1}^{J} L_j(D_j^n)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E03","v141-b-g02-E01","v141-b-g02-E02"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Social-welfare allocations are constrained by charging-efficiency-adjusted minimum and maximum demand, each discharging PHEV's maximum supply, transmission-efficiency-adjusted pairwise supply-demand equality, and nonnegative demand allocations. Scope: Feasible allocations of social-welfare Problem SW within LAGn.","expression":"c_i^{n,\\min} \\le \\eta \\sum_{j=1}^{J} c_{ij}^n \\le c_i^{n,\\max},\\ \\forall i\\in C;\\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{I} d_{ji}^n \\le D_j^{n,\\max},\\ \\forall j\\in Z;\\quad \\rho d_{ji}^n = c_{ij}^n,\\ \\forall i\\in C,\\ \\forall j\\in Z;\\quad c_{ij}^n \\ge 0,\\ \\forall i\\in C,\\ \\forall j\\in Z","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X003","v141-c-R003-X004"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-P001","v141-c-R003-P002","v141-c-R003-P003","v141-c-R003-P004","v141-c-R003-P005"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E04","v141-b-g02-E00"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"This solution requires the energy broker to obtain true and complete information about all PHEVs’ utility and cost functions. Scope: The energy broker's solution of the social-welfare problem using (6) and (7). Condition: When solving SW using (6) and (7).","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E07"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"Since the local charging PHEVs want to maximize their util- ities while the local discharging PHEVs ","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since the local charging PHEVs want to maximize their utilities while the local discharging PHEVs try to minimize their cost, LAGn working as the energy broker not only tries to meet the demand of charging PHEVs, but also maximize electricity allocation efficiency. From a social perspective, the localized P2P electricity trading should maximize social welfare and achieve effective market equilibrium [15]. The energy broker addresses the social welfare maximization problem (denoted as SW) to allocate energy between discharging PHEVs and charging PHEVs. Here, the objective function of social welfare problem is expressed as follows: SW: max_{C^n,D^n} ∑_{i=1}^{I} U_i(C_i^n) − ∑_{j=1}^{J} L_j(D_j^n). (3)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"j i η ij i , , j=1 w th I an dnji ≤Dn,maxj , ∀j ∈Z, au i=1 ρdnji = cnij, ∀i ∈C, ∀j ∈Z, cnij ≥0, ∀i ∈","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Subject to: c_i^{n,min} ≤ η ∑_{j=1}^{J} c_{ij}^n ≤ c_i^{n,max}, ∀i ∈ C, ∑_{i=1}^{I} d_{ji}^n ≤ D_j^{n,max}, ∀j ∈ Z, ρ d_{ji}^n = c_{ij}^n, ∀i ∈ C, ∀j ∈ Z, c_{ij}^n ≥ 0, ∀i ∈ C, ∀j ∈ Z. (4) Here, ρ is average electricity transmission efficiency of the local electricity trading. The objective function in (3) is strictly concave with compact and convex constraints, so there exists a unique optimal solution using Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions [16]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"til- + λijρ = 0. (7) eir eet For the social welfare maximization problem, it is necessary ic- for th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"For the social welfare maximization problem, it is necessary for the energy broker to obtain true and complete information of all PHEVs’ utility and cost functions, and thus to solve the problem using (6) and (7). The complete information of PHEVs includes current energy state, battery capacity and so on, which is private information for PHEVs. However, the PHEVs may not be willing to provide the above private information to the energy broker. To address the issue, the energy broker needs to design a mechanism to extract hidden information from the PHEVs. As each PHEV tries to maximize its own utility, the PHEVs are price taking entities that make the electricity trading market competitive. A double auction is efficient to elicit the hidden information in a real and competitive electricity trading market with enough PHEVs, which is individually rational and weakly budget balanced [17]. The individually rational and weakly budget balanced characteristics of double auction mean that the PHEVs bid truthfully according to privacy information and the energy broker would not lose money to conduct the auction, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"I J A : max [bnij lncnij −snjidnji] (10) Cn ,Dn i=1 j=1 ∂p subject to the same constraints in (4). F","equation_id":"(10)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3) Auctioneer: The bid price matrices from charging and discharging PHEVs are submitted to the auctioneer for performing an iterative double auction mechanism with multiple iterations. The buyers and sellers respectively solve their own optimal electricity buying and selling problems at each iteration to update bid price vectors according to the auctioneer’s newly allocated demand and supply, respectively. The auctioneer solves the following optimal allocation problem A to calculate the amount of traded electricity [18]: A: max_{C^n,D^n} ∑_{i=1}^{I}∑_{j=1}^{J} [ b_{ij}^n ln c_{ij}^n − s_{ji}^n d_{ji}^n ] (10) subject to the same constraints in (4). From (10), if the bid prices of charging and discharging PHEVs are given, the auctioneer can solve Problem A. Note that both Problems A and SW have the same constraints. Problem A is also strictly concave, which ensures that there exists a unique optimal solution for A. For (10), we carry out constraint relaxation through Lagrangian L2. To ensure that the optimal solution of Problem A also solves Problem SW, it is necessary that all KKT conditions along with the stationary conditions are matched for both Problem A and Problem SW. Therefore, L2 and L1 have the same Lagrange multipliers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"3159","section":"V. ITERATIVE DOUBLE AUCTION MECHANISM","anchor_text":"As the KKT conditions are the same, clearly, from (6), (7), (11), and (12), it is known that y- ητcn","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As the KKT conditions are the same, clearly, from (6), (7), (11), and (12), it is known that b_{ij}^n = \\frac{\\eta\\tau c_{ij}^n}{\\left(\\eta\\sum_{j=1}^{J}c_{ij}^n-c_i^{n,\\min}+1\\right)STO_i^n}, (13) s_{ji}^n = 2l_1d_{ji}^n+l_2. (14) Note that, if the bid prices of charging and discharging PHEVs, respectively, satisfy (13) and (14), both the optimal solution of Problem A and Problem SW are the same."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E00","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"3157","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"s, between any charging PHEV and any discharging PHEV in the s- network [14]. A local aggregator LAG","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A set of LAGs (denoted as ι) is indexed by n, where n ∈ ι ≜ {1, 2 . . . n}. Let us denote a set of charging PHEVs in LAGn as υ ≜ (CV_i^n | i ∈ C, n ∈ ι), C = {0, 1, 2, . . . , I}. The discharging PHEVs in LAGn are denoted as ψ ≜ (DV_j^n | j ∈ Z, n ∈ ι), Z = {0, 1, 2, . . . , J}. c_i^{n,min} and c_i^{n,max} are the minimum and maximum of electricity demand for CV_i^n ∈ R in LAGn, respectively. LAGn must provide c_i^{n,min} energy to CV_i^n for normal driving. Here, c_{ij}^n is the electricity demand of CV_i^n for discharging PHEV DV_j^n in LAGn."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"3158 IEEE TRANSA Cni Δ= {cnij|j ∈Z}. In LAGn, the total electricity demand of all th charging PHEVs ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C_i^n ≜ {c_{ij}^n | j ∈ Z}. In LAGn, the total electricity demand of all charging PHEVs is C^n ≜ {C_i^n | i ∈ C}. We define the energy state of CV_i^n before charging as STO_i^n. EV_i^{cap} is the battery capacity of CV_i^n. The satisfaction function of CV_i^n is U_i(C_i^n)=w_i ln( η ∑_{j=1}^{J} c_{ij}^n − c_i^{n,min}+1 ) (1) where η is average charging efficiency from discharging PHEVs to CV_i^n, w_i = τ / STO_i^n is the charging willingness of CV_i^n, and τ is a constant."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-2be1f33ba6-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3158","section":"IV. PROBLEM DEFINITION FOR ELECTRICITY TRADING","anchor_text":"where η is average charging efﬁciency from discharging PHEVs n is the charging willingness of CV in ","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the discharging PHEVs, d_{ji}^n is the amount of electricity supply from discharging PHEV DV_j^n to CV_i^n in LAGn. The electricity supply vector of DV_j^n is D_j^n ≜ {d_{ji}^n | i ∈ R}. In LAGn, the total electricity supply of all discharging PHEVs is D^n ≜ {D_j^n | j ∈ Z}. The maximum of electricity supply for a discharging PHEV in LAGn is D_j^{n,max}. In LAGn, the cost function of DV_j^n is L_j(D_j^n)=l_1 ∑_{i=1}^{I}(d_{ji}^n)^2 + l_2 ∑_{i=1}^{I}d_{ji}^n (2) where l_1 and l_2 are cost factors. Here, l_1 > 0."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-matching","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","quote":"The auctioneer solves the following optimal allocation problem A to calculate the amount of traded electricity [18]:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","quote":"Note that both Problems A and SW have the same constraints."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","quote":"The auctioneer solves the following optimal allocation problem A to calculate the amount of traded electricity [18]:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E13","quote":"Note that, if the bid prices of charging and discharging PHEVs, respectively, satisfy (13) and (14), both the optimal solution of Problem A and Problem SW are the same."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E03","quote":"The energy broker addresses the social welfare maximization problem (denoted as SW) to allocate energy between discharging PHEVs and charging PHEVs."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E03","quote":"From a social perspective, the localized P2P electricity trading should maximize social welfare and achieve effective market equilibrium [15]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E03","quote":"SW: max_{C^n,D^n} ∑_{i=1}^{I} U_i(C_i^n) − ∑_{j=1}^{J} L_j(D_j^n). (3)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E04","quote":"Subject to: c_i^{n,min} ≤ η ∑_{j=1}^{J} c_{ij}^n ≤ c_i^{n,max}, ∀i ∈ C, ∑_{i=1}^{I} d_{ji}^n ≤ D_j^{n,max}, ∀j ∈ Z, ρ d_{ji}^n = c_{ij}^n, ∀i ∈ C, ∀j ∈ Z, c_{ij}^n ≥ 0, ∀i ∈ C, ∀j ∈ Z. (4)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","quote":"For the social welfare maximization problem, it is necessary for the energy broker to obtain true and complete information of all PHEVs’ utility and cost functions, and thus to solve the problem using (6) and (7)."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"b2ec970892991c9561e4323e28eae45bea86b9a8b43456e63b8d0c82502ba948","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The source supplies Eq. (3) as the social-welfare maximization objective and Eq. (4) as its feasible-allocation constraints. Those expressions are transcribed here. The Problem A/SW equivalence and the broker information requirement remain procedural in this patch because this rule does not declare the Problem A bid variables b_ij^n and s_ji^n. The domain of d_ji^n remains ambiguous because D_j^n is defined with i in R while Eq. (4) uses i in C. B evidence is still unreviewed under the experiment policy.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"C^n is the charging-demand allocation optimized in SW and represents local LAG clearing allocations; E001 links clearing.local to clearing.social_welfare."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"D^n is the discharging-supply allocation optimized in SW and represents local LAG clearing allocations; E001 links clearing.local to clearing.social_welfare."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X003","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"c_ij^n is an individual local demand-allocation entry used in the SW feasible set; E001 links local clearing to the social-welfare objective."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X004","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"d_ji^n is an individual local supply-allocation entry used in the SW feasible set; E001 links local clearing to the social-welfare objective."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-P001","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"eta is a fixed efficiency parameter in the SW allocation constraints on local demand."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-P002","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"rho is a fixed transmission-efficiency parameter in the SW local supply-demand equality."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-P003","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"c_i^n,min is a fixed demand lower-bound parameter in the SW feasible set."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-P004","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"c_i^n,max is a fixed demand upper-bound parameter in the SW feasible set."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-P005","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare","clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"D_j^n,max is a fixed supply upper-bound parameter in the SW feasible set."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The formal expression is the social-welfare objective located at clearing.social_welfare, the decision anchor for this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The formal expression lists the feasible-allocation constraints for Problem SW; the constraint member is clearing.social_welfare."}],"revision_requests":["Clarify the domain of d_ji^n: the source defines D_j^n = {d_ji^n | i in R}, but R is not defined in the supplied text, while Eq. (4) uses i in C.","If C002 is to be fully formalized, declare the Problem A bid variables b_ij^n and s_ji^n and the Problem A objective/feasible-set context within this rule; currently they are referenced only through (13)-(14).","B evidence for this rule remains unreviewed under the experiment policy; independent source audit is still required."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
